[Event "Sicilian Defence: the Kan itself"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/0Jbuvv6d/fM8MXH4Z"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Today we learn the Sicilian. Two quiet pawn moves, and then you punch back. }
1. e4 { He grabs the middle. Answer sideways: your c pawn goes two squares. [%cal Yc7c5] } 1... c5 (1... e5 { A fine move, and the whole open game. But today we go sideways. } 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 { That is the Spanish, and a very long story. }) (1... e6 { The French. Solid, and we have a whole file for it already. } 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 { A good defence, but not this one. }) (1... c6 { The Caro-Kann. Also good, also another day. } 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { Solid stuff. The Sicilian fights harder. }) (1... Nc6 { The knight comes out, but it stands on your own c pawn. } 2. d4 d5 3. e5 { He builds a big centre and you have no lever. }) 2. Nf3 { He develops and eyes d4. Open a door for your bishop: pawn to e6. [%cal Ye7e6] } 2... e6 (2... Nc6 { Just as good, and the engine likes it a shade more. } 3. Bb5 e6 4. O-O { That invites the bishop to b5. A different course. }) (2... d6 { The Najdorf road. Perfectly sound, and a mountain of theory. } 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 { A whole other book. Ours is lighter. }) (2... g6 { The dragon fianchetto. Fine, but it is not our set up. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 { A sharp world with its own homework. }) (2... a6 { The right idea, one move too early. } 3. c4 { He clamps the middle before you have a foot in it. }) 3. d4 { There it is. Take the pawn: your c pawn eats his d pawn. [%cal Yc5d4] } 3... cxd4 (3... d5 { Grabbing in the middle, but he takes first and you get checked. } 4. exd5 exd5 5. Bb5+ { Annoying, and he stands better. }) (3... a6 { Too slow. The middle is the thing right now. } 4. d5 { He shuts the door in your face. }) (3... Be7 { A quiet move while the middle is on fire. } 4. Nc3 a6 5. Be3 { He keeps the big centre and you sit cramped. }) 4. Nxd4 { He takes back. Now the little move that names our line: pawn to a6. [%cal Ya7a6] } 4... a6 { The Kan. It takes b5 away from his pieces and gets you ready to push. [%csl Gb5] } (4... Nc6 { Just as good by the numbers. That is the Taimanov, its own study. } 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 { Fine chess. Ours keeps the pawn moves simple. }) (4... Nf6 { Natural, but the knight gets shoved around. } 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Nxc6 { He trades and pushes. Playable, and not our plan. }) (4... Qb6 { The queen goes hunting far too early. } 5. Be3 a6 6. Nd2 { She has to come back and he gained time. }) (4... d6 { Solid, but it shuts your dark bishop in. } 5. c4 Nf6 6. Nc3 { He gets the big clamp for free. }) 5. Bd3 { His bishop takes aim at your king side. Bring yours out swinging: bishop to c5. [%cal Yf8c5] } 5... Bc5 { It leans on his knight and stares at f2. He has to deal with it. [%cal Gc5d4,Gc5f2] } (5... Nf6 { Natural, and just as playable. But the bishop wants that diagonal first. } 6. O-O d6 7. c4 { Fine, though he grabs the space you wanted. }) (5... Qc7 { A good Sicilian square for the lady. } 6. O-O b6 7. Re1 { Perfectly sound. Ours puts the bishop first. }) (5... Ne7 { The engine likes this too, heading for g6. } 6. O-O Nbc6 7. c3 { Also fine. The bishop to c5 is our road. }) (5... Be7 { Too modest. On c5 the bishop bites; on e7 it only watches. } 6. O-O d6 7. c4 { He takes the whole middle in peace. }) 6. Nb3 { He shoves the knight at your bishop. Tuck it into the corner: bishop to a7. [%cal Yc5a7] } 6... Ba7 { The corner is the one square where nothing can ever touch it. [%csl Ga7] } (6... Be7 { The bishop goes home. Safe, and the diagonal is gone. } 7. Qg4 g6 8. O-O { He got a free swing at your king side. }) (6... Bb6 { Nearly right, but a7 is the square where nothing reaches it. } 7. Nc3 d6 8. Bd2 { His pieces still bother the bishop there. }) (6... Bb4+ { A check that only helps him. He blocks and develops. } 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. Qg4 { And now your g pawn is hanging. Not a happy check. }) (6... Bf8 { All the way home. Two moves thrown away. } 7. c4 Nc6 8. Nc3 { He built the clamp while you shuffled. }) 7. Qe2 { The queen backs up the e pawn. Open your bishop with the little pawn to d6. [%cal Yd7d6] } 7... d6 (7... Ne7 { The engine's own pick, heading for g6. Also very good. } 8. Be3 O-O 9. Nc3 { Sound. We free the bishop with the pawn first. }) (7... Nc6 { Just as good. The knight has a fine home there. } 8. Be3 Nge7 9. Nc3 { Fine chess. Our move opens the bishop. }) (7... e5 { It grabs space, but it leaves a hole on d5. } 8. Be3 Bxe3 9. Qxe3 { He will park a knight on d5 forever. }) (7... Qc7 { The lady steps out before the pawns are settled. } 8. Qg4 Kf8 9. Qe2 { Your king cannot castle and he lost nothing. }) 8. O-O { He tucks his king away. Bring the last knight out: knight to f6. [%cal Yg8f6] } 8... Nf6 (8... Nc6 { Just as good. Both knights want a job. } 9. Be3 Nf6 10. c4 { The same house by another door. }) (8... Bd7 { The engine's quiet pick. Perfectly sound. } 9. Be3 Bxe3 10. Qxe3 { Fine, but the knight was the piece in a hurry. }) (8... e5 { Too soon. The hole on d5 opens before you are ready. } 9. Be3 Bxe3 10. Qxe3 { He aims a knight straight at d5. }) (8... Nd7 { The wrong knight, and it blocks your bishop. } 9. c4 Ngf6 10. Nc3 { You will want the other one on f6 anyway. }) 9. c4 { He clamps the middle. Do not let him have it: push your pawn to e5. [%cal Ye6e5] } 9... e5 { The middle is yours and every piece has a home. That is the Kan. [%csl Gd4,Ge5] } (9... O-O { Sensible and good. Castling is never wrong. } 10. Be3 Bb6 11. Nc3 { Also fine. Our pawn grabs the middle first. }) (9... Nc6 { The engine's pick, and every bit as good. } 10. Be3 O-O 11. Nc3 { Sound. We stake the middle with the pawn. }) (9... Nbd7 { Fine too. The knight is heading for e5. } 10. Nc3 Ne5 11. Be3 { Also good. The pawn does the job faster. }) (9... Qc7 { Playable, but it does nothing about the middle. } 10. Nc3 Nbd7 11. Be3 { He is comfortable and you still have to push. }) 0-1


