{ Same spot, but now he just develops the knight. Remember what a6 was for. }
5. Nc3 { A normal developing move. Your little a pawn was preparing something. What? } 5... b5 { Yes! The pawns roll on the queen side and grab room. [%cal Gb5b4,Gb5c4] } (5... Qc7 { The engine's own pick, and a fine move. } 6. Qf3 Nf6 7. Be2 { Solid. But a6 was played to push the b pawn. }) (5... Nc6 { Good and normal. } 6. Be3 Nf6 7. a3 { Fine chess, though the pawn push is our idea here. }) (5... Bb4 { The pin, but his queen swings out and hits you. } 6. Qf3 d5 7. exd5 { He is fine and your bishop is loose. }) (5... d6 { Solid and slow. Now he charges on the other wing. } 6. g4 b5 7. a3 { You pushed anyway, one move later and worse off. }) 6. Bd3 { He develops at your king. Now the long diagonal: which piece wants it? } 6... Bb7 { The bishop stares all the way down at his e pawn. [%cal Gb7e4] } (6... Qb6 { Just as good, leaning on his knight. } 7. Nf3 Qc7 8. O-O { Fine too. The bishop on the long road is our plan. }) (6... d6 { Slow. He hits the pawns before they get going. } 7. a4 b4 8. Na2 { The march is broken up. }) (6... Qc7 { Sound, but the bishop was the piece in a hurry. } 7. O-O Nc6 8. Nxc6 { He trades and you are a beat behind. }) (6... Ne7 { The knight goes the wrong way while the pawns wait. } 7. a4 b4 8. Nce2 { He cracks the queen side open. }) 7. O-O { His king is safe. Bring a knight out and lean on his one in the middle. } 7... Nc6 (7... b4 { Pushing on is just as good. } 8. Na4 Nc6 9. c3 { Also strong. We bring the knight out first. }) (7... Qb6 { The queen crowds her own pieces here. } 8. Nb3 Qc7 9. Re1 { Nothing gained and the knight was better. }) (7... Bd6 { The bishop wants that square, but the knight comes first. } 8. Re1 Nc6 9. Nxc6 { Same trade, and you lost a move. }) (7... h6 { A quiet breath, and he takes over the middle. } 8. Re1 Nc6 9. Nxc6 { You played the knight anyway, a move later. }) (7... Qc7 { A good Sicilian square, but the knight was in more of a hurry. } 8. Re1 Be7 9. e5 { Playable, and his pawn gets rolling first. }) (7... d6 { Careful: your b pawn has no guard yet. } 8. a4 bxa4 9. Rxa4 { The queen side comes under fire at once. }) (7... Ne7 { A slow route. That knight wants c6, and this is the long way. } 8. f4 Nec6 9. Nb3 { Two moves to reach the square. }) 8. Nxc6 { He trades. Careful now: which one takes back? [%csl Rb7,Rd7] } 8... Bxc6 { The bishop, of course. It keeps the long diagonal alive. [%cal Gc6e4] } (8... dxc6 { The pawn takes, and your beautiful bishop is buried. } 9. e5 Qc7 10. Re1 { The diagonal is shut and he owns the middle. }) 0-1


