he queen a guard. } 5... e6 (5... Nc6 { The engine's own choice, and just as good. } 6. Be2 cxd4 7. cxd4 { Fine chess. Ours keeps the shape we know. }) (5... cxd4 { Trading early lets his knight into the game. } 6. cxd4 e5 7. Nc3 { His knight arrives with tempo on your queen. }) (5... Bg4 { A real pin, but he can break it and hit your c pawn. } 6. Nbd2 Nc6 7. dxc5 { He wins a pawn back with pieces flying. }) (5... Bf5 { Nice square, but the pawn move was the useful one. } 6. Be2 e6 7. Na3 { His knight finds the c4 square. }) 6. Be2 { A quiet developing move. Now ask his centre the question. } 6... cxd4 (6... Nc6 { The engine's pick, and every bit as good. } 7. O-O cxd4 8. Nxd4 { You trade anyway, one move later. }) (6... Be7 { Fine, and the pawn trade was the point. } 7. O-O cxd4 8. cxd4 { The same place by another road. }) (6... a6 { A wasted breath. His centre is the target. } 7. O-O cxd4 8. Nxd4 { He got a free move out of it. }) 7. cxd4 { He takes back and his pawn stands alone on d4. Finish developing. [%csl Gd4] } 7... Be7 { Every piece has a home and his lonely d pawn is the thing to nibble at. } (7... Qd6 { Just as good. The queen steps out of the way. } 8. Nc3 Nc6 9. O-O { Also fine. The bishop was the simpler move. }) (7... Nc6 { Also equal. } 8. O-O Qd8 9. Nc3 { The queen has to move soon anyway. }) (7... Qa5+ { A check that helps him develop. } 8. Nc3 Be7 9. a3 { He blocks with gain and you played the bishop anyway. }) 0-1


