{ He brings the knight out instead of the king knight. Play the moves you know. }
2. Nc3 { A different order, and no reason to change your plan. What is our first pawn move? } 2... e6 { Just the same. The pawn steps up and your bishop has a road. } (2... Nc6 { Equally good, and a completely different game. } 3. Nf3 e5 4. Bc4 { That is the closed Sicilian with knights. Ours is simpler. }) (2... a6 { The right idea in the wrong order. } 3. Nf3 e6 4. d4 { You played the pawn anyway. Little pawn moves come second. }) (2... d6 { Solid, but it locks your dark bishop behind the pawns. } 3. Nge2 e5 4. Nd5 { His knight lands on the hole and you cannot chase it. }) (2... g6 { A fine fianchetto, and a whole other course. } 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. d4 { Sharp, and not the set up we are learning. }) 3. Nf3 { Now the other knight. He is heading for the same big centre. Your turn. } 3... a6 { Right again. The little pawn takes b5 away and gets you ready to push. [%csl Gb5] } (3... Nc6 { Good, but it is the other system. } 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 { Playable, and a different set of plans to learn. }) (3... d6 { Solid and slower. } 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 { He gets a free swing on the king side later. }) (3... Nf6 { The pawn simply chases it away. } 4. e5 Nd5 5. Nxd5 { Your pawns get damaged for nothing. }) (3... b6 { The bishop wants the long road, but a6 comes first. } 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 { A tempo behind our line. }) 4. d4 { There is the push. You know exactly what to do. } 4... cxd4 { Take it. Now he recaptures and you are in our main line, with a6 already in. } (4... b5 { The pawn in the middle was the emergency. } 5. d5 Bb7 6. g3 { He shuts the door and your bishop bites on stone. }) (4... Be7 { Far too slow with the middle on fire. } 5. Be3 Qc7 6. d5 { He owns the whole board. }) (4... Qc7 { The queen can wait. The pawn cannot. } 5. d5 d6 6. Bf4 { The middle is his and you are squashed. }) 0-1


