{ The knight is out, and now a big swing on the king side. This is the Grand Prix. }
3. f4 { He wants pawns rolling at your king. But look what he did not do: guard the middle. } 3... d5 { Perfect. He spent his move on the wing, so you take the centre. [%cal Gd5e4] } (3... Nc6 { Equally good, and the pawn comes next anyway. } 4. Nf3 d5 5. Bb5 { The same idea, one move later. }) (3... Nf6 { The knight walks into the pawn storm. } 4. Nf3 Be7 5. d4 { He rolls forward and you have no lever. }) (3... a6 { Too slow against a charge. Hit the middle instead. } 4. Nf3 b5 5. d4 { He builds the big centre for free. }) (3... Nh6 { Odd but playable. The pawn is the point though. } 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. Bb5 { You get away with it, but the break was cleaner. }) 4. Nf3 { He develops. Bring a piece out and lean on the middle. } 4... Nc6 (4... d4 { Pushing on. Also good, and a bit loose. } 5. Bb5+ Nd7 6. Ne2 { Equal, but the knight was the calmer move. }) (4... Be7 { Fine, though the knight guards more. } 5. d4 Nf6 6. e5 { He gets to push you back. }) (4... dxe4 { Trading away your own good pawn. } 5. Nxe4 Nc6 6. Bb5 { He is happy: the middle is open for his pieces. }) (4... a6 { Still too slow while the middle is in the air. } 5. d4 cxd4 6. Nxd4 { He solved his problem for free. }) 5. Bb5 { The bishop pins your knight to the king. Careful now: which piece comes out? [%csl Rc6] } 5... Ne7 { The right one. The king knight comes to e7, guarding c6 a second time. [%csl Gc6] } (5... Nf6 { The natural square, but he trades and pushes. } 6. Bxc6+ bxc6 7. e5 { Your pawns are wrecked and the knight has to run. }) (5... d4 { Pushing on now lets him wreck the pawns. } 6. Bxc6+ bxc6 7. Nb1 { He will pick the weak pawns off later. }) (5... Bd6 { Developing into the storm. } 6. d4 Bd7 7. Be3 { He opens the middle while your king sits there. }) (5... dxe4 { The knight on c6 is pinned and undefended. } 6. Ne5 Bd7 7. Bxc6 { He collects a piece. }) 6. exd5 { His pawn takes and now it attacks your knight. Deal with it. [%csl Rc6] } 6... exd5 { Take back with the pawn. The middle stays full and everything is guarded. [%csl Gd5] } (6... Nxd5 { Also fine, but he gets to smash your pawns. } 7. Bxc6+ bxc6 8. Ne5 { Equal, and much uglier to play. }) (6... Bd7 { Your knight is hanging! } 7. dxc6 Nxc6 8. O-O { A whole piece for one pawn. }) (6... Qc7 { The knight on c6 is still under the pawn. } 7. dxc6 b6 8. d4 { A piece gone and nothing to show for it. }) (6... a6 { The pawn on d5 comes first. } 7. Bxc6+ bxc6 8. dxe6 { He wins a pawn and cracks your pawns open. }) 0-1


