{ This time a quiet little pawn, making a house for his bishop. The closed Sicilian. }
3. g3 { Slow and solid. And again he has left the middle alone. You know the answer. } 3... d5 { Yes. Every time he goes slowly, you go to the middle. [%cal Gd5e4] } (3... Nc6 { Fine, and the pawn is still the move. } 4. Nge2 d5 5. exd5 { Same plan, one move later. }) (3... Be7 { Solid and slow, just like him. } 4. Nge2 Nc6 5. Bg2 { He finishes his house in peace. }) (3... d6 { One square short of the good one. } 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. d4 { Now he takes the middle himself. }) (3... a6 { Useful someday. The middle is now. } 4. Nf3 d6 5. d4 { He got the big centre for free. }) 4. Bg2 { The bishop moves into its house and looks straight down at your pawn. } 4... d4 { Push past it! The pawn kicks his knight and blocks the bishop at the same time. [%csl Rc3][%cal Gd4g1] } (4... dxe4 { Trading opens the road for exactly the bishop you want to block. } 5. Nxe4 Be7 6. d3 { His bishop stares right through the board at you. }) (4... Nf6 { Natural, but the pawn charges and you get pushed back. } 5. e5 Nfd7 6. f4 { He grabs the space and your knight is in a corner. }) (4... Ne7 { Playable, and the push was the punch. } 5. exd5 exd5 6. d3 { Level, but he is comfortable now. }) (4... Nc6 { Careful: after the trade his knight comes and takes on d5. } 5. exd5 exd5 6. Nxd5 { A clean pawn gone. }) 5. Nce2 { The knight shuffles sideways. His pieces are tangled. Bring one out. } 5... Nc6 (5... e5 { The engine's own pick, building a wall of pawns. } 6. f4 Bd6 7. d3 { Just as good. The knight comes out first for us. }) (5... Bd6 { Also strong, aiming at his king side. } 6. f4 e5 7. d3 { Fine too. Pieces before pawns is the safer habit. }) (5... d3 { Giving the pawn back to open lines. Playable, but greedy the wrong way. } 6. cxd3 Nc6 7. Nf3 { He untangles and the pawn is just gone. }) (5... Be7 { A shade passive while his knights are stuck. } 6. d3 Nc6 7. f4 { He gets to sort himself out. }) 6. d3 { He props things up. Now build the wall behind your proud pawn. } 6... e5 { Two pawns abreast, his knight is stuck, and his bishop stares at granite. [%csl Gd4,Ge5] } (6... Bd6 { Just as good. } 7. f4 f6 8. Nf3 { Also comfortable. The pawn wall is the cleanest picture. }) (6... Nf6 { Playable, and the pawn was the point. } 7. f4 e5 8. Nf3 { You pushed it anyway, a move later. }) (6... f6 { It guards e5 but leaves your king airy. } 7. f4 Bd6 8. Nf3 { The pawn could just go there itself. }) (6... a5 { A useful little move, but the middle was calling. } 7. f4 e5 8. Nf3 { Same wall, one move slower. }) 0-1


